description:
Found by the selfie-0cb2 plan reviewer; sharpened by the team lead on verification.

crates/cli/src/commands/common.rs has TWO call sites reading dotfiles_directory(), and they disagree about what a missing directory means.

create_dotfile_service (fn at :58), lines 79-80:

    let dotfiles_dir = config.selfie_config().dotfiles_directory();
    if dotfiles_dir.is_dir() {
        ... attach repo ...
    }

No else, no warning, no event. The service is built without a dotfiles repository and every standalone dotfile silently vanishes from apply, drift and list. The user is told nothing.

The other site, line 162, gets it right:

    if !dotfiles_dir.is_dir() {
        display.print_error(format!("Dotfiles directory does not exist: {}", ...));
        display.print_suggestion(format!("Create it with: mkdir -p {}", ...));
        return 1;

So the correct behavior already exists ~80 lines away in the same file. This is a defect, not an unconsidered case, and the fix has a local model to copy — though the diagnostic must reach the event system rather than stdout when it originates below the CLI boundary.

Easy to hit by accident: dotfiles_directory() (crates/selfie/src/config.rs:84-91) defaults to a SIBLING of package_directory, so relocating the package directory without also passing --dotfiles-directory points it at a path that usually does not exist.

Also one of three candidate mechanisms for the '0 deployed, 8 skipped' output that prompted selfie-0cb2.

Same family as selfie-hat (config loader silently dropping unknown keys) and selfie-wwc.

Found by the config-diagnostics unit author (one site), extended to four by the team lead.

crates/mcp-server/src/server.rs has four copies of the same silent drop:

  :270   if dotfiles_dir.is_dir() { ... }
  :325   let dotfiles_repo = if dotfiles_dir.is_dir() { ... } else { None };
  :694   if dotfiles_dir.is_dir() { ... }
  :743   let dotfiles_repo = if dotfiles_dir.is_dir() { ... } else { None };

No else that reports, no event, no diagnostic. When dotfiles_directory is missing or misconfigured, standalone dotfiles are invisible to every MCP dotfile tool and the tool still returns a success shape — so an AI assistant reasons over an incomplete map and presents it as authoritative. That is worse than the CLI equivalent, where a human at least sees the directory listing they expected.

SAME FAMILY as selfie-3kpg, which inventoried seven sites in the CLI. That inventory was CLI-only, so these four were never counted. Full family is eleven sites.

Fix should apply the same rule the CLI unit adopted: warn when dotfiles_directory was EXPLICITLY configured and is missing, stay silent when the sibling default is merely absent — SelfieConfig::configured_dotfiles_directory() (added by that unit) is the accessor. Threading it through SelfieServer::new is the work, which is why it was deliberately left out of that unit's scope.

Note two of the four sites (:325, :743) mirror the CLI's name-collision pattern; check whether either feeds a uniqueness check before deciding severity — see selfie-qux6 for how that plays out on the CLI side.

Found by the selfie-hat/3kpg unit author; verified by the team lead.

crates/cli/src/commands/dotfiles/list.rs:110-112:

    Err(_) => {
        // Non-fatal — standalone dotfiles dir is optional
    }

The comment justifies the silence on the grounds that the directory is optional. The arm catches every error the load can produce — a parse failure, a permission denial, an IO error, and any refusal added later — not just 'the directory is not there'. A user whose standalone dotfiles fail to load sees a listing that silently omits them.

Distinct from selfie-3kpg, which is about the missing-directory case reported by an is_dir() check a few lines earlier at :100. This is an error swallow on the load itself, and fixing 3kpg does not fix it.

Third instance of the same shape in this codebase; see selfie-kldo (deps.rs:238 and two spec commands) where a comment claiming 'if it doesn't exist, silently skip' sits above an else arm catching every error. The pattern is a comment describing the intended case while the code catches all of them — .claude/rules/comments.md calls this a comment defending a drift rather than describing a design.

Deliberately left out of the selfie-3kpg unit's scope: list.rs is outside config/CLI-config, and the fix is an error-classification change rather than a missing diagnostic.

Found by the selfie-h2kr plan reviewer and author; verified by the team lead.

Three sites treat 'could not load this package' as 'this package does not exist', and tell the user the latter.

1. crates/selfie/src/package/service/deps.rs:235-240

    // Try loading the package — if it doesn't exist, silently skip.
    let Ok(package_blob) = repo.get_package(package_name) else {
        visit_state.remove(package_name);
        return Ok(());
    };

The comment claims the skip is for a missing package. The else arm catches every error get_package can return — parse failure, IO error, permission denial, and any refusal added later. This is a comment defending a drift rather than describing a design.

2. crates/cli/src/commands/spec/remove.rs:32

    let Ok(package_blob) = repo.get_package(package_name) else {
        display.print_error(format!("Package '{package_name}' not found."));
        return 1;

Tells the user the package is not found for a file that plainly exists and that they can see in the directory.

3. crates/cli/src/commands/spec/edit.rs:18 — .ok(), same effect.

Widens with every refusal class added. selfie-h2kr adds an irregular-file refusal, so a fifo named <package>.yml will be reported as 'Package not found' by spec remove and spec edit, and silently skipped by dependency resolution.

Fix by distinguishing the not-found case from a load failure and reporting the rest. The information is available — get_package returns a typed error.

Found by the selfie-h2kr plan author, verified by the team lead.

crates/selfie/src/fs/filesystem.rs:159 documents the contract as:

    /// A fifo, socket, device node or directory. `None` for a regular file, ...

crates/selfie/src/fs/real.rs:66 says the opposite, in a block comment with rationale:

    // A **directory** is deliberately not one of these, though it is not a regular
    // file ... `a_directory_at_the_target_is_an_ordinary_error` pins that it stays an
    // ordinary error ... Folding directories in here refuses that entry earlier

The trait contract contradicts every implementation of it.

This is worse than a doc nit for two reasons. Per .claude/rules/comments.md a trait's docs ARE the contract, so a caller reading the trait is entitled to rely on directories being refused — and none are. Second, the likely 'fix' is backwards: someone reconciling the two will change the IMPLEMENTATION to match the doc, which breaks the named test and moves a refusal earlier in the deploy path, changing which error a user sees for a directory at a dotfile target.

Fix the doc, not the code. State that directories are excluded and why, on the trait, since the exclusion is contract rather than mechanism.

VERIFIED at 4ef9c05. crates/selfie/src/paths.rs:20-37. The Component::ParentDir arm pops only when parts.last() is a Component::Normal:

    Component::ParentDir => {
        // Only pop Normal components; never pop past root or a prefix.
        if matches!(parts.last(), Some(Component::Normal(_))) {
            parts.pop();
        }
    }

When parts is EMPTY -- a leading '..' -- it pops nothing and also pushes nothing, so the component vanishes. '../../etc/passwd' normalizes to 'etc/passwd'.

The doc comment (paths.rs:17-18) says it 'processes components left to right, popping on  and skipping ' and contrasts itself with canonicalize. Nothing says a leading '..' is DISCARDED rather than preserved. The inline comment explains the guard as 'never pop past root or a prefix', which is the intent -- but the implementation achieves it by dropping the component instead of keeping it, and those differ for any relative input.

NOT a live vulnerability, and the bead should not be read as one:
- On the target path, expand_target_path (fs/target.rs:140) normalizes the raw target. '../../etc/passwd' becomes 'etc/passwd', which is still relative and is refused by the absoluteness guards. Refused either way.
- On the containment path, is_within (paths.rs:41+) reasons about base_dir.join(candidate). '/pkgs/../../etc/passwd' normalizes with a non-empty parts vec, so the pops are real and the result '/etc/passwd' is correctly reported outside '/pkgs'. Not bypassed.

The problem is that the function's NAME and DOC promise pure lexical normalization while the behavior is lossy for a case nobody wrote down, and .claude/rules/secrets.md leans on 'All package-relative source paths go through crate::paths containment' as a load-bearing guarantee. A future caller reasoning about containment from a bare relative path -- rather than from a joined absolute one -- would inherit the drop silently. That is the same shape as the invariant-stated-but-not-enforced problem verification.md describes.

Fix directions, in order of cost: document the behavior on normalize_path and say why dropping is correct for its callers; or preserve a leading '..' so the output is a faithful lexical normalization and let each caller reject it (note this would change expand_target_path's output for that input, though not the accept/reject outcome); or split into a total lexical normalizer plus a separate containment-oriented one.

Found by the Unit 1 plan reviewer (second reviewer) while checking the target rule's handling of './x' and '../x'.

VERIFIED by the Unit 2 plan reviewer against 14daa1a. crates/selfie/src/dotfile_service/service.rs:252-254:

    let yaml = serde_saphyr::to_string(state).map_err(|e| {
        FileSystemError::IoError(std::sync::Arc::new(std::io::Error::other(e.to_string())))
    })?;

A failure to SERIALIZE selfie's own deploy state -- a bug in selfie or in serde-saphyr, with nothing to do with the filesystem -- is wrapped as FileSystemError::IoError. The caller then renders it as 'Failed to save deploy state: <io error text>', sending the user to check permissions and disk space for a condition neither can cause.

Low priority: DeployState is a HashMap<String, DeployEntry> of plain strings, so to_string realistically cannot fail today. That is precisely why it is worth typing correctly rather than leaving as a lie that is currently unobservable -- the same argument selfie-13bq and selfie-u36f resolved by deleting a field nothing read.

Fix direction: give the error type a variant that says serialization failed, or return a different error type from save_deploy_state entirely. Check what the callers do with it first -- both currently only render it into a warning.

Found while reviewing the selfie-y8ub load-side error handling; the save side has the mirror-image problem of conflating two conditions, where the load side conflated three.

crates/mcp-server/src/server.rs:757-765 hand-rolls an error result as:
  {"status": "error", "message": ...}

while the event-stream path for the same tools produces, via event_collector.rs:33-35:
  {"status": "failure", "error": ...}

Two shapes, two status strings ('error' vs 'failure'), two field names ('message' vs 'error'), for the same concept on the same tool. An AI consumer has to handle both, and the tool descriptions do not say so.

Cosmetic in that nothing is incorrect, but the MCP server's whole job is presenting a predictable structured surface to an assistant, and .claude/rules/architecture.md notes tool descriptions are written specifically to guide AI assistants.

Fix direction: pick one shape and route both exits through it.

Found by the selfie-cvpm plan author (U4 of the command-execution batch).
